+### [Displaced Parallel Projection for Stereoscopic Volume Rendering](18500-displaced-parallel-projection-for-stereoscopic-volume-render.md)
+A viewing geometry for parallel stereoscopic imaging where two view points (Left Center of Projection LCoP and Right Center of Projection RCoP) are separated by distance $e$. One center is at the origin, the other at $(e \cos(\theta/2), 0, e \sin(\theta/2))$. This setup allows the second half of a stereo pair to be generated from the first by exploiting coherence, reducing rendering time by 70-80%.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
 *   **Time Complexity:** The compositing step is linear with respect to the number of non-transparent voxels and pixels, but significantly faster in practice due to skipping transparent and occluded regions [S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires $O(N)$ memory for the volume RLE, where $N$ is the number of voxels. Specifically, 4 bytes per non-transparent voxel and 1 byte per run [S2]. The algorithm requires three copies of the volume RLE in memory for optimal performance [S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires $O(N)$ memory for the volume RLE, where $N$ is the number of voxels [S2]. Specifically, 4 bytes per non-transparent voxel and 1 byte per run [S2]. The algorithm requires three copies of the volume RLE in memory for optimal performance [S2].
 *   **Measured Performance:**
     *   On an SGI Indigo R4000, the algorithm renders a $128 \times 128 \times 109$ 'brainsmall' dataset in an average of 0.4 seconds [S2].
